Spec: main

Purpose:
  Be the `operator` bin half of the crate: one straight-line startup — hand-built tokio
    runtime, rustls `ring` provider pin, an inline tracing subscriber (not the dead
    @telemetry::@init), `vynil-core` wiring, @manager::@Manager::new, an actix listener on
    `0.0.0.0:9000` serving diagnostics, health and Prometheus text, then one @tokio::@select!
    racing the four controller futures against the server until the first completes. The file
    owns startup order, the three route answers and the process fate; the client, the watches
    behind the futures and the metric bodies come from the specs referenced below.

Owns:
  ./main.rs

References:
  ./lib.sdd — the lib half, pulled wholesale by `pub use controller::*`; its
    @get_client_name-vs-@common::@get_client_name client-name scenario is the identity split
    this startup freezes with its @wire_core_k8s call.
  ./manager.sdd — pending; @manager::@Manager owns the @kube::@Client build, the four CRD
    probe panics, the base context, the futures this file only races, and the @String and
    @Diagnostics bodies the routes here hand out.
  ./metrics.sdd — content of the `/metrics` body; this file renders, never encodes.
  ./telemetry.sdd — @init is dead: this file rebuilds its subscriber inline with a different
    fmt style and different double-install fate; @telemetry::@init_tracer is a private fn even
    the telemetry half here could not reach.
  ./instance_common.sdd, ./jukebox.sdd, ./instanceservice.sdd, ./instancesystem.sdd,
    ./instancetenant.sdd — pending; the @Action retry/backoff of the raced futures lives in
    those reconciles and error policies.
  /common/src/context.sdd — @init_k8s (observable no-op), @wire_core_k8s first-wins
    registration, executable-derived @common::@get_client_name.
  /common/src/lib.sdd — @Error and @Result: this file's sole @Error::@Other text and the @Debug
    shape @main prints on the exit-1 lane; no stable unique id (Tasks).
  /server/src/main.sdd — sibling bin startup frozen with a provider-pin debug line and
    @std::process::@exit lanes; differs from this one on both counts.

Must:
  Carry exactly three crate attributes, in this order:
    `#![cfg_attr(not(test), deny(clippy::unwrap_used, clippy::expect_used))]`
    `#![cfg_attr(not(test), warn(clippy::arithmetic_side_effects, clippy::indexing_slicing))]`
    `#![allow(unused_imports, unused_variables)]`
    The blanket allow masks this bin's genuinely dead imports @HttpRequest and the @web
    self-binding — no @unwrap or @expect site exists in the file; the two failure macros it
    does use are bare @panic! calls, which the deny does not cover.
  Name the bin surface `pub use controller::*`: the @Manager, @Error, @Result and @telemetry
    names the handlers and @async_main use all resolve through the `controller` lib
    root; the bin target is `operator` (../Cargo.toml `[[bin]]`), never imported itself.
  Run @main synchronously — no #[tokio::main] — building its runtime by
    @tokio::@runtime::@Builder::@new_multi_thread, then @enable_all, then @thread_stack_size of
    `8 * 1024 * 1024` bytes, commented
    `kube 2.0 + async_trait : state machines en debug sont larges` while /Cargo.lock pins kube
    `3.1.0`; @build @Err is @panic! `failed to build Tokio runtime: {e}`; the sole body
    statement is @block_on of @async_main, and @main hands its @Result to the std
    Termination exit.
  Run @async_main as exactly one pass, no loop or retry, in this order: rustls provider pin →
    tracing build and @set_global_default install → @common::@context::@init_k8s() →
    @common::@context::@wire_core_k8s() → @manager::@Manager::new awaited → actix factory +
    bind @shutdown_timeout → @tokio::@select!.
  Pin rustls before any kube client exists — @rustls::@crypto::@ring::@default_provider then
    @install_default with the @Result discarded by @ok(): silent on success, silent on the
    already-set @Err (unlike the sibling /server/src/main.sdd debug line), never verifying the
    effective provider is `ring`; the in-file comment blames rustls `0.23.41` provider
    autodetect loss against kube's client-creation-time TLS config.
  Install as the process subscriber a hand-built stack — @tracing_subscriber::@fmt::@layer at
    full defaults (stdout, UTC microsecond timestamps, ANSI colour unless @NO_COLOR is
    non-empty), no `.compact()`, plus the registry-level @EnvFilter chain of
    @try_from_default_env falling through @try_new(`"info"`) to @unwrap_or_else
    @EnvFilter::@new(`"info"`) — layered into @Registry::@default and installed through
    @tracing::@subscriber::@set_global_default, @panic! with
    `failed to install the global tracing subscriber: {e}` on a duplicate install; @telemetry::@init
    is never called and @init_k8s contributes nothing.
  Keep the `telemetry`-feature half dead even for the bin: gated by
    `#[cfg(feature = "telemetry")]`, a @tracing_opentelemetry::@layer with @telemetry::@init_tracer — a `fn` private to the lib
    module, so the bin-side cfg build could not name it (E0603) even before the 0.32 API
    breakage ./telemetry.sdd freezes; the feature defaults off, no build config enables it,
    and @OPENTELEMETRY_ENDPOINT_URL therefore belongs to main's env surface only on a lane
    that cannot compile.
  Wire core exactly once before the first client use: @wire_core_k8s registers the five
    `vynil-core` hooks whose client supplier runs on this runtime and whose name supplier is
    the executable-derived @common::@get_client_name — for the shipped binary named `operator`
    that is `controller.vynil.solidite.fr`, the same string as this crate's own
    @get_client_name @PatchParams lane, so the divergence ./lib.sdd freezes stays invisible in
    production.
  Treat the four @BoxFuture values destructured in tuple order JukeBox, TenantInstance,
    SystemInstance, ServiceInstance as lazy handles: nothing polls — no watch starts, no
    reconcile runs — until @tokio::@select! after a successful bind, and a bind failure drops
    all four unpollled; the watch order, `any_semantic` configs and @kube per-kind
    @error_policy backoff belong to @manager::@Manager::new and the kind modules, as do its
    `limit=1` CRD probe @panic! texts `cannot create the Kubernetes client: {e}` and
    `is a CRD installed? {e}` which cross this file only as unwinds reaching exit `101`.
  Build one actix @HttpServer from a @move factory: fresh @App per worker with
    @app_data @Data of @manager::@Manager::clone (Arc-shared diagnostics and metrics
    unchanged by the clone), @wrap of @middleware::@Logger::@default with @exclude of
    `/health`, services @index, @health and @metrics registered in that order — each route's
    method and path are fixed by the @get values `/`, `/health`
    and `/metrics`; @bind the literal `0.0.0.0:9000` else @map_err to @Error::@Other formatting
    `can not bind to 0.0.0.0:9000: {e}` and `?` out, then @shutdown_timeout to `5` seconds.
  Race five branches in one @tokio::@select!, first completion winning and every other branch
    dropped mid-poll: the four controller arms log `JukeBox controller exited`,
    `TenantInstance controller exited`, `SystemInstance controller exited` and
    `ServiceInstance controller exited` at @warn — reachable only when a watched stream ends,
    because each kind's @error_policy @Action retry keeps it endless otherwise — while
    @server.run's @Result is discarded by the `_` binding and logged as @info `actix exited`
    whether it stops clean or errors; actix default signal stop (@disable_signals never
    called) drains workers within the 5-second @shutdown_timeout before that arm can fire, and
    a controller arm firing skips any drain.
  End @async_main with @Ok after any arm and @main with it unchanged: runtime drop, exit
    status `0`, no completion line beyond the winning arm's single log line.

Exposes:
  @main — `fn @main() -> @Result` of unit: the `operator` bin target entry; a bin is not
    importable, so its surface is the sequence and exit statuses pinned in Must, Returns and
    Raises.
  @async_main — private @async fn holding that sequence.
  Handlers @metrics, @health and @index — @actix_web @get attributes on `/metrics`, `/health`
    and `/`, registered only here; @metrics and @index extract @app_data @Data of @Manager.

Accepts:
  No argv parsing, no flags, no config file, no stdin: @main takes nothing and this file
    contains no @std::@env::@var at all. Its direct env surface is exactly `RUST_LOG` — read
    once at @EnvFilter::@try_from_default_env — and `NO_COLOR`, read per event by the default
    fmt ANSI path; `OPENTELEMETRY_ENDPOINT_URL` belongs only to the dead telemetry cfg.
  Indirectly, values owned by others: the startup envs @manager::@build_context reads
    (`CONTROLLER_BASE_DIR`, `VYNIL_NAMESPACE`, `AGENT_IMAGE`, `AGENT_ACCOUNT`,
    `AGENT_LOG_LEVEL`, `TENANT_LABEL`) and cluster discovery through the @kube client belong
    to ./manager.rs and @common, never rendered by this file, only racing what they produce.
  Preconditions the file trusts: the literal `0.0.0.0:9000` bindable; @Manager present in
    @app_data — registered in the factory, with @metrics and @index relying on actix's default
    error response if that ever changed.

Returns:
  OS exit codes are the only value leaving the process: `101` whenever any @panic! wins — the
    runtime builder, @manager::@Manager::new on client or CRD probes, or an unwinding
    reconcile — message on stderr; `1` only via @main's @Err @Result on @bind failure, where
    std renders `Error: ` plus the @Error @Debug, keeping the @thiserror variant name, so
    stderr reads `Error: Other("can not bind to 0.0.0.0:9000: …")`; `0` for every @select!
    arm, signal stop and early controller death alike, each followed by @Ok, runtime drop and
    no further output.
  Three route answers, status always @Ok: `GET /metrics` → `200`, body =
    @manager::@Manager::metrics verbatim with @content_type
    `application/openmetrics-text; version=1.0.0; charset=utf-8`, even when the encoder error
    is logged downstream and the body lacks families or `# EOF`; `GET /health` → `200` JSON
    string `"healthy"`; `GET /` → `200` JSON of @Diagnostics — the @Utc @DateTime `last_event`
    field alone (@reporter is `#[serde(skip)]`), written by the reconciler, never by this
    handler; unmatched paths and methods take actix defaults.

Raises:
  @Error::@Other — the one failure this file constructs: @bind rejection wrapped as
    `can not bind to 0.0.0.0:9000: {e}` and returned up @main, ending the process at `1` with
    no tracing line; the text carries no stable unique id (Tasks). The @panic! texts owned
    here are `failed to build Tokio runtime: {e}` and
    `failed to install the global tracing subscriber: {e}` — the second is dead in shipped
    builds, since this is the process's first and only subscriber install. Everything else is
    transit: @manager probe panics and reconcile unwinds pass through @block_on to the same
    `101` fate, message on stderr, no cleanup.

Handles:
  Provider answer, both arms: installed-first @Ok and already-set @Err both drop through
    @ok() — no branch in file ever logs, checks, or selects the provider.
  Subscriber answers: fresh @Ok is the only reachable arm of the install, a second-install
    @Err @panic!.
  `RUST_LOG` unset → fallback `info`; malformed value → `info` too; set-but-empty →
    directive-less @EnvFilter, the whole registry silent, fallback skipped — same
    tracing-subscriber behaviour ./telemetry.sdd pins, through the live operator path.
  @select! losers: dropped futures get no await, no cleanup and no graceful drain of in-flight
    reconciles, whatever the winning arm was; @manager::@Manager and its Arc state just drop.
  `NO_COLOR` honoured per event, unset, empty and non-empty values all compile-safe.
  Access lines: @middleware::@Logger only excludes `/health`, so `/` and `/metrics` produce
    access records through the `log` facade whether or not any subscriber captures them (Tasks).

Tasks:
  [ ] Cover this bin once: the file holds no #[cfg(test)] and nothing spawns the binary, so all
      five Scenarios rest on reading alone; a harness must launch the built `operator` with a
      cluster fixture and assert the stdout/stderr lines, the three `9000` answers, and the
      exit codes — `0` for every @select! arm, `1` only on the @bind lane, `101` for the panic
      and runtime-builder lanes.
  [?] This startup duplicates @telemetry::@init rather than calling it and lands on a different
      style — full fmt layer vs `.compact()`, @panic! on double install vs silent @install drop —
      and the feature half is a use of @init_tracer the lib never exports; decide later
      which file owns the operator bootstrap and delete the loser.
  [?] Mixed failure styles: @manager and runtime builder @panic! (`101`) while @bind returns
      @Err through @main's @Result (`1`, @Debug on stderr only, no tracing); decide later one
      fatal path, one exit class, one channel for operator startup.
  [?] A future ending on any of the four arms drops the other three reconciles and the listener
      and still exits `0`; a crash loop then looks identical to a clean stop from inside
      Kubernetes. Decide whether early arms should drain, log more loudly, or exit nonzero.
  [?] The @middleware::@Logger records flow through the `log` facade while this file installs
      its subscriber via @set_global_default rather than the builder @init that wires the
      log→tracing bridge, so `/` and `/metrics` access lines likely reach nothing; decide later
      (install and assert) or drop the middleware.
  [?] @common::@context::@init_k8s is an empty `const fn`; calling it changes nothing here
      either. Decide together with /common/src/context.sdd whether the no-op call stays.
  [?] The crate-wide `#![allow(unused_imports, unused_variables)]` hides two dead actix imports
      (@HttpRequest, @web self-binding); decide later to drop the allow with the imports.
  [?] `can not bind to 0.0.0.0:9000: {e}` carries no stable unique id against the project error
      rule. Decide later with the @Error policy in /common/src/lib.sdd.
  [?] The @thread_stack_size comment blames `kube 2.0` while /Cargo.lock pins kube `3.1.0`;
      decide whether the comment is stale or the sizing rationale still holds.

Done when:
  Every Scenario maps to a green process-level test or a decided `[?]`, the startup-failure
    style and bootstrap-ownership decisions are recorded, and `cargo test -p operator`,
    `cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings`, `cargo +nightly fmt --check` and
    `specdd lint` are green.

Scenario: A good boot answers on 9000 and idles
  Given a reachable cluster with all four CRDs installed and `0.0.0.0:9000` free
  When the `operator` binary starts
  Then stdout carries no boot banner at all — @main and @async_main log nothing themselves —
    while the three routes answer `200`: `/metrics` as the four-registry OpenMetrics body,
    `/health` as `"healthy"`, `/` as the one-key JSON holding `last_event`
  And the four watches poll the cluster without any further route call or log line from this
    file, and the process keeps running with the @select! unresolved
  No mapped test exists.

Scenario: Early controller exit drops every neighbour and exits 0
  Given one of the four futures reaching stream end — a watcher terminal failure or a stopped
    @Action that ends the stream
  When its kind-specific @warn line lands
  Then the other three reconciles mid-poll and the actix listener drop within the same
    @select! poll, no @shutdown_timeout drain runs, and stdout carries only that one @warn line
  And @async_main returns @Ok with `actix exited` never printed, and the exit status is `0`
  No mapped test exists.

Scenario: Signal stop drains and exits 0
  Given boot completed and a request mid-flight
  When SIGINT or SIGTERM reaches the process
  Then actix stop handling drains within @shutdown_timeout `5`, @server.run resolves, @info
    `actix exited` reaches stdout, the four @BoxFuture drop unpollled-clean, @async_main
    returns @Ok and the exit status is `0`
  No mapped test exists.

Scenario: A port clash takes the Result lane to 1
  Given `0.0.0.0:9000` already bound by another listener
  When @bind fails after @Manager::new listed every CRD
  Then @Error::@Other ends @main's @Result, stderr shows one line of the @Error @Debug
    rendering — `Error: Other("can not bind to 0.0.0.0:9000: …")` — no tracing record mentions
    it, and the exit status is `1`
  Then none of the four futures was ever polled, so no watch touched the cluster
  No mapped test exists.

Scenario: RUST_LOG and NO_COLOR answer through this path
  Given `RUST_LOG` unset then set to an invalid directive then set to the empty string, and
    `NO_COLOR` set non-empty in a parallel boot
  When @EnvFilter::@try_from_default_env and the fmt layer read them at boot
  Then the first two fall back to global `info`, the empty string registers zero directives
    and the registry logs nothing, and @NO_COLOR keeps every line plain
  No mapped test exists.

Example:
  line shape this file alone produces: `<ts> WARN operator: ServiceInstance controller exited`
    — @warn from @async_main, not from any module
  stderr text on the bind lane: `Error: Other("can not bind to 0.0.0.0:9000: <io error>")`
    — @Debug rendering, and this file writes nothing further there
  startup stdout of a healthy boot: only the subscriber's own lines the reconcilers add
    through the manager and kinds — @main adds none itself
